{
  "wireMagic": "pb3",
  "pens": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wsWinStyles": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wpWinPositions": [ {
  
  } ],
  "events": [ {
    "tStartMs": 229,
    "dDurationMs": 4661,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Thanks to LEGO – presenting LEGO City - for their support of PBS Digital Studios."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 4890,
    "dDurationMs": 2680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Humanity’s future is glorious."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 7570,
    "dDurationMs": 5730,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "As we master space travel, we’ll hop from\none cold dead world to the next. Terraforming as we go."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 13300,
    "dDurationMs": 6060,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Life will blossom in our path and eventually the galaxy will shimmer with beautiful Earth-like orbs."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 19360,
    "dDurationMs": 1880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "I mean... maybe."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 21240,
    "dDurationMs": 7840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Sounds a little science-fictiony. But it wouldn't sound so far fetched if we proved we could do it at least once."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 29080,
    "dDurationMs": 2980,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "If we successfully terraformed Mars."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 37240,
    "dDurationMs": 3900,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We already have the technology to bring humans to Mars and to set up small settlements"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 41140,
    "dDurationMs": 2960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "- or at least we could do within a generation."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 44100,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But those settlements will need to be cocooned\n- shielded against the deadly cold, the intense radiation"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 50500,
    "dDurationMs": 2840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and the fatal lack of atmospheric\npressure."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 53340,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Surely if we want to thrive on Mars – to\nturn it into our second home – these settlers,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 59190,
    "dDurationMs": 6210,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "or their descendants, will need to be able\nopen the airlocks, shed their spacesuits,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 65400,
    "dDurationMs": 2810,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and step out onto a survivable surface."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 68210,
    "dDurationMs": 5270,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We’ll need to terraform Mars, as our first\nstep to terraforming the galaxy."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 73480,
    "dDurationMs": 5820,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Terraforming Mars has long been a science\nfiction dream – from Kim Stanley Robinson’s"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 79300,
    "dDurationMs": 6580,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Mars trilogy to Total Recall to the Red Faction\ngame series to Elon Musk’s Twitter feed."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 85880,
    "dDurationMs": 2420,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But what would it really take?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 88300,
    "dDurationMs": 3460,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "How science-fiction-y is the whole concept\nof terraforming?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 91760,
    "dDurationMs": 2740,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In the end it’s a question of atmosphere."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 94500,
    "dDurationMs": 7440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Mars’ current atmospheric pressure is 0.6%\nthat of Earth – and that means circulatory shutdown"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 101940,
    "dDurationMs": 3240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "within a minute for an unprotected humans."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 105180,
    "dDurationMs": 2610,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But it also means almost no greenhouse effect."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 107790,
    "dDurationMs": 5660,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Light from the Sun, which is already fainter\ndue to Mars’ distance – is radiated directly"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 113450,
    "dDurationMs": 1810,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "back out into space."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 115260,
    "dDurationMs": 5740,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "On Earth that same light first bounces around\nin our thick atmosphere, heating it up."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 121000,
    "dDurationMs": 4500,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "At an average of -60 Celsius, water freezes\non Mars."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 125500,
    "dDurationMs": 5160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But even if the planet were warmer, liquid\nwater would still be impossible in that thin"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 130660,
    "dDurationMs": 4480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "atmosphere – it sublimates directly from\nice to gas."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 135140,
    "dDurationMs": 5460,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And of course Earth’s atmosphere protects\nus from harmful cosmic rays and the most dangerous"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 140600,
    "dDurationMs": 2080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "ultraviolet radiation from the Sun."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 142680,
    "dDurationMs": 4220,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "All of that bad stuff has a direct path to\nthe Martian surface."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 146900,
    "dDurationMs": 5080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So, the most important step in terraforming\nMars is to give it an atmosphere –"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 151980,
    "dDurationMs": 2820,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "ideally as close to Earth’s as possible."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 154800,
    "dDurationMs": 6280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In the imaginations of sci-fi writers all\nwe need to do is unlock the planet’s latent potential."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 161080,
    "dDurationMs": 5980,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "After all, Mars WAS once a warmer, watery\nplanet with a much thicker atmosphere."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 167060,
    "dDurationMs": 5500,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "I mean, that's conclusive – our rovers and orbiters\nhave found incontrovertible evidence"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 172560,
    "dDurationMs": 2740,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of an ancient watery surface."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 175300,
    "dDurationMs": 5480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The hope then, is that this water and the\natmosphere that once supported it is now all"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 180780,
    "dDurationMs": 3200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "locked in the planet’s crust and ice caps."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 183980,
    "dDurationMs": 1960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We just need to release it."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 185940,
    "dDurationMs": 5780,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Surely we can just nuke the poles, melt enough\ncarbon dioxide and water vapor to kickstart"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 191720,
    "dDurationMs": 4920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a feedback cycle of greenhouse warming and that'll release more gases… and voila!"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 196900,
    "dDurationMs": 1600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Earth 2.0"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 198660,
    "dDurationMs": 2100,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "OK, not so fast."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 200769,
    "dDurationMs": 6271,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "There’s a real risk that Mars actually lost\nits atmosphere to space, rather than absorbed"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 207040,
    "dDurationMs": 1540,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "it into the surface."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 208580,
    "dDurationMs": 4980,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The issue is that the planet is relatively\npuny."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 213560,
    "dDurationMs": 5100,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "At 11% the mass of Earth, it has a weaker\ngravitational field that grips less tightly"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 218660,
    "dDurationMs": 1220,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to an atmosphere."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 219880,
    "dDurationMs": 5500,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And that small size means that the Martian core\ncooled down more quickly than Earth’s core,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 225380,
    "dDurationMs": 4199,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "solidifying long ago and shutting down its global magnetic field."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 229580,
    "dDurationMs": 5240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Earth’s magnetic field protects us from\nthe solar wind, as we saw in a recent episode."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 234820,
    "dDurationMs": 5759,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The unprotected and loosely bound Martian\natmosphere may have been slowly shaved away"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 240580,
    "dDurationMs": 2700,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "by that wind over billions of years."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 243280,
    "dDurationMs": 3060,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And in fact that is exactly what happened."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 246340,
    "dDurationMs": 5820,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The ablation of what is left of the Martian\natmosphere has now been directly observed by NASA’s"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 252160,
    "dDurationMs": 3680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "MAVEN spacecraft, as we’ve also discussed\nbefore."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 255840,
    "dDurationMs": 5340,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And the lack of atmospheric material in the\ncrust has been confirmed pretty conclusively"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 261180,
    "dDurationMs": 2060,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "by observations of the Martian surface."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 263240,
    "dDurationMs": 5480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In a nice Nature Astronomy article last year,\nplanetary scientists Bruce Jakosky and Christopher"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 268720,
    "dDurationMs": 6200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Edwards calculate the plausibility of using\nthe remaining surface carbon dioxide to replenish"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 274920,
    "dDurationMs": 6720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the Martian atmosphere, based on observations of NASA’s Mars Reconnaissance Orbiter and Mars Odyssey spacecraft."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 281660,
    "dDurationMs": 4300,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "They focus on CO2 because it’s the only\nplausible greenhouse molecule in any significant"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 285960,
    "dDurationMs": 1780,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "abundance on Mars."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 287740,
    "dDurationMs": 5720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "They assess whether release of the accessible\nCO2 reserves could get Mars anywhere near"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 293460,
    "dDurationMs": 1520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Earth’s atmospheric pressure."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 294980,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And… unfortunately they conclude that no\nnear-future technology could hope to to kickstart"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 301220,
    "dDurationMs": 3220,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the recovery of any useful atmosphere."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 304440,
    "dDurationMs": 1920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But, you know what?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 306360,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Let’s go ahead and run the numbers real\nquick, because maybe something is still possible."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 311080,
    "dDurationMs": 4320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "After all, these researchers only ruled out\nNEAR future technology."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 315400,
    "dDurationMs": 1260,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "What about medium future?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 316700,
    "dDurationMs": 980,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Far future?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 317680,
    "dDurationMs": 3720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So there are 3 broad sources for CO2 on Mars."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 321400,
    "dDurationMs": 6180,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "First there’s the south polar icecap – which\nconsists of water ice several kilometers deep,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 327580,
    "dDurationMs": 4980,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "interspersed with thick layers of CO2 ice\n– discovered by radar soundings with the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 332560,
    "dDurationMs": 2150,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Mars Reconnaissance Orbiter."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 334710,
    "dDurationMs": 4840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "If all the polar CO2 were released, it would\nmaybe double the current amount of CO2 in"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 339550,
    "dDurationMs": 6150,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the atmosphere – which is a factor of around\n100 times too low to make a difference."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 345700,
    "dDurationMs": 4740,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And by the way, that CO2 couldn’t be released\nwith nukes alone - it’s too deep."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 350440,
    "dDurationMs": 1040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Sorry, Elon."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 351480,
    "dDurationMs": 7320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The second accessible source is CO2 absorbed\ninto the surface dust – the regolith - up to 100m deep."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 358820,
    "dDurationMs": 5580,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Unlike, for example, Earth’s permafrost,\nthis stuff wouldn’t just melt under global warming."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 364400,
    "dDurationMs": 6100,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It would shift in its equilibrium over\n10,000 years to release a small fraction of its CO2."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 370540,
    "dDurationMs": 5640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "At any rate, even if we managed to heat the entire\nregolith across the entire Martian surface"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 376190,
    "dDurationMs": 4220,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "we’d only get 4% of the Earth’s atmospheric\npressure."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 380410,
    "dDurationMs": 4250,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The final source is carbonate minerals in the crust."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 384660,
    "dDurationMs": 5560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "These carbonates would need to be mined and\nprocessed by heating to around 300 Celsius."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 390220,
    "dDurationMs": 6940,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But complete strip-mining of even the largest carbonate\nsurface deposits on Mars probably get you less carbon"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 397160,
    "dDurationMs": 2140,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "than melting the polar ice caps."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 399300,
    "dDurationMs": 3180,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So much for near-future accessible carbon."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 402480,
    "dDurationMs": 6520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But those carbonate minerals probably\nexist in much larger quantities deep beneath the surface."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 409020,
    "dDurationMs": 5260,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And that’s really our only hope to find\nenough CO2 - or really any native Martian"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 414280,
    "dDurationMs": 3100,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "material - to replenish the atmosphere."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 417380,
    "dDurationMs": 3360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Let’s do a quick calculation to see what\nit would take."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 420740,
    "dDurationMs": 6060,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "First, let’s pretend there’s an accessible\nlayer of limestone – calcium carbonate – across"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 426800,
    "dDurationMs": 1880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the entire surface of Mars."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 428680,
    "dDurationMs": 3420,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "There isn’t, but hey, we’re dreamers."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 432100,
    "dDurationMs": 7680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We need about 10,000 kg of material per square\nmeter to duplicate Earth’s atmospheric pressure."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 439780,
    "dDurationMs": 3580,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Seriously, that’s how much atmosphere is\nabove your head right now."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 443360,
    "dDurationMs": 2300,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "No wonder it’s so hard getting out of bed\nin the morning."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 445660,
    "dDurationMs": 7460,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "High density limestone is 2500 kg/m^3 and\nyields 44% of its mass in CO2"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 453120,
    "dDurationMs": 2260,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "when heated or exposed to acid."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 455380,
    "dDurationMs": 6780,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So to get 10 tons of CO2 for every square\nmeter on the surface of Mars you’d have to dig down"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 462160,
    "dDurationMs": 3340,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "over 10 meters – across the entire\nplanet!"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 465510,
    "dDurationMs": 3100,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That’s a few quadrillion tons of rock."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 468610,
    "dDurationMs": 2810,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "I hope you have your diamond pickaxe ready."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 471420,
    "dDurationMs": 5480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In reality of course we’d need to first\nlocate and then dig down some kilometers before"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 476900,
    "dDurationMs": 2700,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "we could access most of the carbonates."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 479600,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Extracting such a quantity from depth is hard\nenough, but let’s think about processing it."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 484960,
    "dDurationMs": 5220,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We can either heat the carbonates to hundreds\nof degrees Celsius or use acid to dissolve"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 490180,
    "dDurationMs": 1380,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "out the CO2."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 491560,
    "dDurationMs": 6780,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We’d need to process around 20% of all Martian\nwater via electrolysis to get that acid."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 498340,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The electrolysis path might be better because it would\ngive us oxygen as a byproduct of making that acid."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 504340,
    "dDurationMs": 6800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The energy cost in both cases is similar, though – several septillion joules."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 511140,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Several thousand times the total annual energy\nconsumption of the entire Earth."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 516980,
    "dDurationMs": 2960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That’s definitely sounding far-far future."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 519940,
    "dDurationMs": 3120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But not quite impossible."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 523060,
    "dDurationMs": 5160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Finally we actually have a picture of what\nterraforming Mars would actually look like."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 528230,
    "dDurationMs": 3500,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Let’s say we want to finish the work in\na single generation."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 531730,
    "dDurationMs": 6570,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We’d need to cover much of the surface of\nMars in solar cells made from abundant silicon"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 538300,
    "dDurationMs": 5880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in the crust, or build 10 or so million gigawatt\nfusion power plants."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 544180,
    "dDurationMs": 2360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "There’s really no other viable energy source."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 546540,
    "dDurationMs": 5000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We’d need to channel this energy deep into\nthe crust to power vast hoards of robotic"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 551540,
    "dDurationMs": 5160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "miners-slash-processing plants, meanwhile\npumping water from the icecaps across the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 556700,
    "dDurationMs": 1660,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "entire globe."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 558360,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This could get us a carbon dioxide-oxygen\natmosphere in a few decades, or in centuries"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 563640,
    "dDurationMs": 4120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "… or millenia if you scale down the power\nsupply to something less insane."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 567760,
    "dDurationMs": 5780,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Nonetheless, our descendants could see a Mars\nwith sufficient air pressure and greenhouse"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 573540,
    "dDurationMs": 4120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "effect to allow liquid water to persist on\nthe surface."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 577660,
    "dDurationMs": 4400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Now, Mars actually does have enough water for a few\nlakes and rivers."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 582060,
    "dDurationMs": 6120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The ice cap water would cover the entire surface\nto about 30 meters – which is not enough"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 588180,
    "dDurationMs": 2980,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to start a proper water cycle or have oceans,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 591160,
    "dDurationMs": 3220,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "but there may be a lot more water deeper in\nthe crust."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 594380,
    "dDurationMs": 1700,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We’d better hope so."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 596080,
    "dDurationMs": 4900,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Our brand new CO2-oxygen atmosphere is not exactly earth-like."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 600980,
    "dDurationMs": 3340,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In fact, it’s instantly and fatally toxic\nto humans and animals,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 604320,
    "dDurationMs": 2480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and not so great for plant life."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 606800,
    "dDurationMs": 6920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Certain algaes can survive in a pure CO2 atmosphere\n–which is handy, because blue-green algae"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 613720,
    "dDurationMs": 5100,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "– cyanobacteria - was responsible for first\noxygenating Earth’s atmosphere."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 618820,
    "dDurationMs": 4900,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And we’ll need that photosynthesis because\notherwise oxygen will be quickly leeched from"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 623720,
    "dDurationMs": 3160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the atmosphere as it oxidizes the surface."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 626880,
    "dDurationMs": 5700,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So, there’s our next snapshot of the far future of a terraformed Mars –"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 632580,
    "dDurationMs": 6180,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "brand new oceans green with photosynthesizing, probably genetically-engineered, slime."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 638760,
    "dDurationMs": 5720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And perhaps eventually a breed of post-humans\ngenetically or even cybernetically adapted"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 644480,
    "dDurationMs": 2100,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to deal with a CO2 atmosphere."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 646580,
    "dDurationMs": 4000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "I just described the “easy” path to building\nan atmosphere on Mars."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 650660,
    "dDurationMs": 3900,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It may be the only way to do it only using\nMartian materials."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 654580,
    "dDurationMs": 5700,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Variations are possible - like introducing\n“super” greenhouse gases like CFCs."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 660280,
    "dDurationMs": 3620,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But that still doesn’t give us the needed\natmospheric pressure."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 663900,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "At any rate, to get a true Earth-like atmosphere\nwe need a non-toxic filler molecule."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 669740,
    "dDurationMs": 1780,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "CO2 sucks."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 671520,
    "dDurationMs": 6500,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Nitrogen is much better - it works great on Earth\nanyway, but Mars has very little of the stuff."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 678020,
    "dDurationMs": 5480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "To really build an Earth-like atmosphere we\nhave to turn our eyes to the rest of the solar system."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 683500,
    "dDurationMs": 3920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "One popular idea is just to smack some comets\ninto Mars."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 687420,
    "dDurationMs": 7360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Comets contain tons of frozen volatiles – gas-forming\nmolecules like CO2, H20 and the presence of"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 694780,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "molecular nitrogen in comets was only recently\nconfirmed by the Rosetta mission."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 700540,
    "dDurationMs": 1800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But how many comets do we need?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 702340,
    "dDurationMs": 5220,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Well, assuming comets contain an amount of\nnitrogen similar to the composition of the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 707560,
    "dDurationMs": 6260,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "pre-solar nebula then can guess that around\n5% of a comet’s mass is nitrogen."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 713820,
    "dDurationMs": 4900,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That gives the typical medium-to-large comet\na hundred billion tons of the stuff."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 718720,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So, to build a quadrillion-ton nitrogen atmosphere\nthat’s, like, 10,000 comets."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 725200,
    "dDurationMs": 3720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "O-kay, so we’re still in far-future la-la\nland."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 728920,
    "dDurationMs": 6300,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But it’s actually not significantly less\ncrazy, or more crazy, than melting the Martian surface."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 735220,
    "dDurationMs": 2660,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "What would THIS effort look like?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 737880,
    "dDurationMs": 6780,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Imagine this - a vast fleet of robotic spacecraft\nswarming the Kuiper belt, nudging its plentiful"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 744660,
    "dDurationMs": 4700,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "iceballs in just the right way to send them\nplowing towards Mars."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 749360,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Hopefully with exquisite aim, otherwise Earth\nis in for a pounding, also."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 754560,
    "dDurationMs": 4939,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It would presumably take centuries to put\nsuch a fleet in place, and more centuries"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 759500,
    "dDurationMs": 2480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to “de-orbit” those comets."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 761980,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Once Mars has been suitably bombarded there’s\nstill a lot of work tweaking the new atmosphere."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 768060,
    "dDurationMs": 4480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The good news is that those comets brought\nwith them a LOT of water,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 772540,
    "dDurationMs": 2980,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "so we have deep global oceans at this point."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 775520,
    "dDurationMs": 840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "OK."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 776360,
    "dDurationMs": 2900,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Let’s fast-forward several centuries."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 779260,
    "dDurationMs": 5119,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Mars has an atmosphere – either released\nfrom deep in the crust or brought in from"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 784379,
    "dDurationMs": 2430,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the far outer solar system."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 786809,
    "dDurationMs": 3270,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The last step is to protect the new atmosphere."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 790079,
    "dDurationMs": 5341,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We canNOT restart Mars’ magnetic field – to\ndo that we’d have to re-melt the entire"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 795420,
    "dDurationMs": 720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "core."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 796140,
    "dDurationMs": 3260,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But we can try to build an external magnetic\nshield."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 799400,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The easiest would be to do that in space – an\norbiting field generator placed between Mars"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 805080,
    "dDurationMs": 2760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and the Sun, like a giant space umbrella."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 807840,
    "dDurationMs": 5700,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The resources and energy needed to build this\nis insane – but hey, we just built an atmosphere,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 813540,
    "dDurationMs": 1580,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "so why not?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 815120,
    "dDurationMs": 3460,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Honestly, all of this is pretty insane."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 818620,
    "dDurationMs": 1520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And frankly, unlikely."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 820140,
    "dDurationMs": 6800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Would we really muster the resources to terraform\nMars if we can’t do the same to re-terraform"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 826940,
    "dDurationMs": 1460,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Earth?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 828400,
    "dDurationMs": 2380,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But there is another option."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 830780,
    "dDurationMs": 3680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Why build a sky if we can build a roof?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 834460,
    "dDurationMs": 4100,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Instead of terraforming – what if we paraterraform."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 838560,
    "dDurationMs": 3580,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Build what is known as a worldhouse."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 842140,
    "dDurationMs": 3800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We could cover vast tracts of land with an\nairtight bubble."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 845940,
    "dDurationMs": 3120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Or, more likely, many many connected bubbles."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 849060,
    "dDurationMs": 4240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "These would be tall enough to encapsulate\nentire cities, and importantly –"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 853300,
    "dDurationMs": 2900,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "plenty of Earth-like natural wilderness."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 856200,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Oh, and I’m still a proponent of centrifuge\ncities – mag-lev rotating habitats to simulate"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 862440,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Earth gravity. Also shown rather beautifully\nin this more practical design by James Telfer."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 868680,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "If we wanted to cover, say, 10% of the Martian\nsurface with a 300 meter tall worldhouse,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 874760,
    "dDurationMs": 5620,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "it'd require several orders of magnitude less material than building an entire atmosphere."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 880380,
    "dDurationMs": 3420,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So, say a handful of comets and/or the polar ice caps"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 883800,
    "dDurationMs": 4460,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "should be enough to fill our worldhouse with air\nand water."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 888260,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Now, without a real atmosphere, space radiation\nis gonna be a problem for our worldhouse,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 893540,
    "dDurationMs": 3300,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "as is the constant bombardment of micro-meteors."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 896840,
    "dDurationMs": 5740,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "People who live in glass houses shouldn’t\nthrow stones, nor live under a stone-throwing universe."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 902580,
    "dDurationMs": 5960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But perhaps there are advanced or just very,\nvery thick materials that would serve."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 908540,
    "dDurationMs": 6180,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So there’s our final image of humanity’s\nfuture on Mars: thousands of city-sized bubbles"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 914720,
    "dDurationMs": 2640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "spread across the still-barren landscape."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 917360,
    "dDurationMs": 6540,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And inside each bubble an oasis – a lush,\nsnow-globe replica of old Earth."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 923910,
    "dDurationMs": 7119,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "However we do it, Mars will surely be our\nfirst step, our proof of concept if we choose"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 931029,
    "dDurationMs": 3911,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that destiny - if we choose to terraform space\ntime."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 934940,
    "dDurationMs": 4540,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Thanks to LEGO – presenting LEGO City - for\ntheir support of PBS Digital Studios."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 939480,
    "dDurationMs": 3080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Can you believe it’s been 50 years since\nwe landed on the moon?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 942560,
    "dDurationMs": 5700,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It started out with one small step for man\nand now the journey to Mars is right around the corner!"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 948300,
    "dDurationMs": 5880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Featuring sets inspired by real aerospace\ntechnology, LEGO City Space aims to inspire"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 954180,
    "dDurationMs": 5580,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "future space explorers to imagine what role\nthey can play to get us to the Red Planet."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 959760,
    "dDurationMs": 3520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "To discover more go to the link in the description."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 963280,
    "dDurationMs": 2620,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So we missed a couple of comment responses."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 965900,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Today I'm going to cover two episodes: The\nepisode \"What Happened Before the Big Bang\","
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 970860,
    "dDurationMs": 5500,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in which we look at eternal inflation. As well\nas the episode on the exciting possibility"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 976360,
    "dDurationMs": 4020,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that the North and South magnetic poles may\nbe about to flip."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 980380,
    "dDurationMs": 4660,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Wabi Sabi asks why the inflaton field is assumed\nto be a scalar field."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 985040,
    "dDurationMs": 3460,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Well, that's a great question - but I'm afraid the answer\nmay not be satisfying."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 988500,
    "dDurationMs": 3040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It's because a scalar field is all you need."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 991540,
    "dDurationMs": 6180,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This is the simplest type of quantum field, consisting\nof only a single scalar value at all points in space."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 997720,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Give such a field a constant energy density\nand you get exponential expansion."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1002440,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But more complex fields like vector fields\nand spinor fields can do the job too -"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1007000,
    "dDurationMs": 5480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and some inflationary models use them, resulting\nin more complex inflation scenarios."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1012480,
    "dDurationMs": 4920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But many physicists argue that you shouldn't\nadd unnecessary complexity, so a scalar field"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1017400,
    "dDurationMs": 2480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "tends to be the default for inflaton."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1019880,
    "dDurationMs": 4940,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Joshua Kahky asks whether the Inflaton Field\ncould also explain Dark Energy."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1024820,
    "dDurationMs": 3440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Well, the answer is yes, possibly."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1028260,
    "dDurationMs": 5560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Inflation supposedly happened because the\ninflaton field had a very high energy density,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1033820,
    "dDurationMs": 4000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and it stopped when that energy dropped to\na very low value."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1037820,
    "dDurationMs": 2980,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But that low value may not have been zero."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1040800,
    "dDurationMs": 4940,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "If the inflaton field was left with a very\ntiny but positive energy density,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1045740,
    "dDurationMs": 6560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "then it's possible that it could now be powering the\ncurrent accelerating expansion that we call dark energy."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1052300,
    "dDurationMs": 4100,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But for that to happen, the inflaton field\nwould have had to have transitioned between"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1056400,
    "dDurationMs": 5900,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "two stable or semi-stable states that are\na factor of 10^27 different in energy."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1062300,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Now, we can try to imagine a single field with\nthat property, or we can imagine two separate fields -"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1067580,
    "dDurationMs": 3980,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It's not clear which of those two imaginings\nis more of a stretch."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1071560,
    "dDurationMs": 4260,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "There were a lot more great questions on eternal\ninflation, but I'll get to those when we do"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1075820,
    "dDurationMs": 3940,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the eternal inflation challenge question answer\nepisode."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1079760,
    "dDurationMs": 6300,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "For now, let's move on to the possible flipping\nof Earth's North and South magnetic poles."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1086060,
    "dDurationMs": 4620,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "EarthKnight points out that while Venus lacks an\nEarth-type intrinsic magnetic field,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1090680,
    "dDurationMs": 5660,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the solar wind striking its atmosphere creates\nan induced magnetic field that does protects the planet."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1096340,
    "dDurationMs": 1560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That's a nice point, EarthKnight."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1097900,
    "dDurationMs": 1580,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It's a very cool effect."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1099480,
    "dDurationMs": 3940,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The solar wind partially ionizes Venus's upper\natmosphere."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1103420,
    "dDurationMs": 4280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Electrical currents are induced and these\nproduce a magnetic field that pushes back"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1107700,
    "dDurationMs": 1940,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "against the Sun's magnetic field."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1109640,
    "dDurationMs": 4700,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Venus's magnetic force-shield isn't nearly\nas strong as Earth's, however, so our Venusian"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1114340,
    "dDurationMs": 4240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "floating cloud cities had better still have\nvery thick roofs."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1118580,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "As many viewers noted, what we currently call\nthe north magnetic pole is technically a south"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1124820,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "magnetic pole - as in, what we would call\nthe south pole of a magnetic dipole or a bar magnet."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1130820,
    "dDurationMs": 6020,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "You know how the magnetic north pole of a bar\nmagnet is attracted to the south pole of a second bar magnet?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1136840,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Well, your compass's north pole is attracted\nto geographic north - which means geographic"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1142440,
    "dDurationMs": 3620,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "north must correspond to a magnetic south\npole."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1146060,
    "dDurationMs": 5480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Nolan Westrich, while laughing in Australian,\nnotes that with the flipping of the magnetic"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1151540,
    "dDurationMs": 3620,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "poles it will be America's turn to be upside\ndown."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1155160,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Well, given that the northern hemisphere is\ncurrently the magnetic south, I think that"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1159800,
    "dDurationMs": 4000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "means that North America, Europe, and most\nof Asia have been at bottom of the world all"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1163800,
    "dDurationMs": 1820,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "this time without realizing it."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1165620,
    "dDurationMs": 4620,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So hold onto something and don't look at the\nsky - it's a long way down."
    } ]
  } ]
}
